+# Deploying the USDC Tip Jar
+
+Two independent artifacts:
+
+1. **`UsdcTipJar` on Base** — one transaction, then contract verification.
+2. **The site** — a static Next.js export pinned on IPFS.
+
+They are coupled in one direction: the contract has to be deployed *before* the site is built, because
+`yarn deploy` writes the jar's Base address into `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts` and the
+bundle bakes it in.
+
+Nothing in this document is run for you. Every command below is meant to be executed by a human who has
+checked what it does first.
+
+## 0. Before you start
+
+| Need                         | Why                                                                    |
+| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| A funded deployer account    | Real ETH on Base for gas (a deployment costs well under $1)             |
+| A Base RPC endpoint          | `foundry.toml` defaults to `https://mainnet.base.org`; use your own for anything serious |
+| An Etherscan API key         | `ETHERSCAN_API_KEY` in `packages/foundry/.env`, for contract verification |
+| [kubo](https://docs.ipfs.tech/install/command-line/) (`ipfs`) | Post-deploy verification pulls the CID back down and diffs it |
+
+Run through the whole local workflow in [README.md](./README.md) first. A Base fork is the same state you
+are about to deploy against, so anything that breaks there will break on Base.
+
+## 1. Deploy the contract to Base
+
+Import or generate the deployer account (this stores an encrypted keystore, not a plaintext key):
+
+```bash
+yarn account:import        # existing private key
+# or
+yarn generate              # fresh account
+yarn account               # show the address and its balance on each network
+```
+
+Fund that address with a little ETH on Base, then deploy:
+
+```bash
+yarn deploy --network base
+```
+
+The script refuses to run on any chain that has no code at the Base USDC address, so a mistyped network
+fails before it spends anything.
+
+Note the deployed address and the block number from the output. `yarn deploy` also updates
+`packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts` with an `8453` entry — that file is what the frontend
+reads, so commit it.
+
+Verify the source on Basescan:
+
+```bash
+yarn verify --network base
+```
+
+Confirm the deployment is what you think it is:
+
+```bash
+JAR=$(jq -r '.transactions[] | select(.contractName == "UsdcTipJar") | .contractAddress' \
+  packages/foundry/broadcast/Deploy.s.sol/8453/run-latest.json)
+BASE_RPC=https://mainnet.base.org   # or your own endpoint
+echo "jar: $JAR"
+
+cast call $JAR "usdc()(address)"     --rpc-url $BASE_RPC   # -> 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+cast call $JAR "owner()(address)"    --rpc-url $BASE_RPC   # -> your deployer
+cast call $JAR "tipCount()(uint256)" --rpc-url $BASE_RPC   # -> 0
+```
+
+(`cast` only resolves the `base` alias from `foundry.toml` when run inside `packages/foundry`, so these
+use the URL directly.)
+
+If `usdc()` is not Circle's USDC on Base, stop and redeploy — the jar's token is immutable.
+
+## 2. Build the site
+
+Set the production environment in `packages/nextjs/.env.local` (see `.env.example`):
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK=base
+NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://<the origin you will serve from>
+NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY=<your key>
+NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=<your project id>
+```
+
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` are baked into the bundle at build time — a static
+export cannot read environment variables at runtime. The two API keys are public by construction; the
+defaults in `scaffold.config.ts` are shared demo keys and will be rate limited.
+
+Then:
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+This runs `next build` with `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true`, which turns on `output: "export"`,
+`trailingSlash` and unoptimized images (see `packages/nextjs/next.config.ts`). The result is a fully static
+site in `packages/nextjs/out`.
+
+### Check the bundle before you pin it
+
+A CID is immutable: whatever you get wrong here you get to publish forever under a new one.
+
+```bash
+# The Base chain id and the jar address are actually in the bundle
+grep -rq 8453 packages/nextjs/out/_next/static/chunks && echo "targets Base ✓"
+grep -riq "${JAR#0x}" packages/nextjs/out/_next/static/chunks && echo "knows the jar ✓"
+
+# No stray localhost URLs in the metadata (that means NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL was not set)
+grep -o 'content="[^"]*localhost[^"]*"' packages/nextjs/out/index.html || echo "no localhost leaks ✓"
+
+# It actually renders
+npx serve -s packages/nextjs/out -l 4173
+curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4173/ | grep -o "<title>[^<]*</title>"
+```
+
+Open <http://127.0.0.1:4173>, connect a wallet on Base, and confirm the feed and the form talk to the
+deployed jar. Then stop `serve`.
+
+> **Serve from the root of an origin.** Next emits root-absolute asset URLs (`/_next/...`), so the bundle
+> works on a **subdomain** gateway (`https://<cid>.ipfs.dweb.link`), on DNSLink, and on ENS — but *not*
+> under a path gateway (`https://gateway/ipfs/<cid>/`), where `/_next/...` resolves above the CID root.
+> A relative `assetPrefix` does not fix it: Next emits the same `./_next/...` on nested routes such as
+> `/debug/`, where it then resolves one level too deep.
+
+## 3. Upload
+
+Either path produces a CID for the `out` directory.
+
+**Via the bundled uploader** (BuidlGuidl's IPFS cluster):
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs
+```
+
+That script rebuilds, uploads `out`, and prints the CID with a subdomain-gateway URL. It is build *and*
+upload in one step — use it only when you have already reviewed the bundle from step 2.
+
+**Or with kubo directly**, if you want to see the CID before anything leaves your machine:
+
+```bash
+# Compute the CID locally first — nothing is published by --only-hash
+ipfs add -rQn --cid-version 1 packages/nextjs/out
+
+# Publish it
+ipfs daemon &
+CID=$(ipfs add -rQ --cid-version 1 packages/nextjs/out)
+echo "CID: $CID"
+```
+
+A CID on your own node only lives as long as that node does. Pin it somewhere durable as well — a pinning
+service (Pinata, web3.storage, Filebase) or your own cluster:
+
+```bash
+ipfs pin remote add --service=<service> --name=usdc-tip-jar $CID
+```
+
+## 4. Post-deploy verification
+
+Run all of these against the CID you just published.
+
+```bash
+CID=<the cid you published>
+```
+
+**The published bytes are the bundle you built.** This is the check that matters; it catches a stale
+build, a partial upload, and an uploader that mangled something.
+
+```bash
+ipfs get $CID -o /tmp/tipjar-published
+diff -r packages/nextjs/out /tmp/tipjar-published && echo "published content matches local build ✓"
+```
+
+**The content is actually retrievable from a public gateway** — i.e. it propagated off your node:
+
+```bash
+curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "root: %{http_code}\n"  "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/"
+curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "debug: %{http_code}\n" "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/debug/"
+
+# a JS chunk, to prove the root-absolute asset paths resolve on this gateway
+CHUNK=$(grep -o '/_next/static/chunks/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]*\.js' packages/nextjs/out/index.html | head -1)
+curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "chunk: %{http_code}\n" "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link$CHUNK"
+```
+
+All three must be `200`. If the root is `200` but the chunk is `404`, you are on a path gateway — see the
+note in step 2.
+
+**The page is wired to the live jar.** In a browser on `https://<cid>.ipfs.dweb.link`:
+
+- the header shows **Base**, not Foundry;
+- the stats show the jar's real balance — cross-check it:
+
+  ```bash
+  cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+    "balanceOf(address)(uint256)" $JAR --rpc-url $BASE_RPC
+  ```
+
+- send one small real tip (0.01 USDC clears `MIN_TIP`) and confirm it lands in the feed and in
+  `cast call $JAR "tipCount()(uint256)" --rpc-url $BASE_RPC`.
+
+**Give it a stable name.** A raw CID changes on every deploy. Point DNSLink or ENS at it so links survive:
+
+```bash
+# DNSLink: TXT record on _dnslink.<your domain>
+dig +short TXT _dnslink.<your domain>     # expect: "dnslink=/ipfs/<cid>"
+
+# ENS: set the contenthash record, then check it resolves
+curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" https://<name>.eth.limo/
+```
+
+## Redeploying
+
+- **Frontend only** (copy, styling, a bug fix): rebuild, re-upload, repoint DNSLink/ENS. The contract and
+  every tip in it are untouched.
+- **Contract change**: a new deployment is a new jar with an empty feed and no balance. Withdraw from the
+  old jar first (`cast send $OLD_JAR "withdraw()"`), then deploy, rebuild the frontend against the new
+  address, and re-upload. There is no migration path for the old feed — it stays readable at the old address.
